Alain:
Dear Pranav,

You cannot use the same analysis on the different outcomes because the model assumptions are not satisfied for all of them. For example, you need to transform eda_tonic with a square root and you cannot use current_comfort_1_7 in a linear model (it is true for ANOVA and LMM). 
I don't understand what you do : 
::	You run a repeated measures ANOVA,
::	You fit a linear mixed models. 
One can get the same answer with repeated-mesures ANOVA and LMM so why do you do both ? Note that the syntax you used for the former analysis works only when the design is perfectly balanced (I think it is the cas after your filter). When the design is unbalanced (I imagine you are in this case since you filter your data on the number of observations for each participant), it is recommended to use an LMM. 
The effect size you compute only works for balanced data in case of valid normality and homoscedasticity assumptions. If not, it is biased. You should certainly use the following definition : 
\eta_2 = SS_effect / SS_total

I really think you should stop using AI that much. It makes your code impossible to read and as said above you cannot repeat the same analyses without looking at assumptions. And don't suppress warnings because it can prevent you from finding something wrong in your model. 
Another advice is to chose some of the outcomes because a reviewer will ask you to take into account the risk of type I error if you do so many models. 


 May you remind me how to determine which participant to keep? I am thinking if we publish urban part and greenery part as two subsets to keep as many measurements as possible. If so, we don't need to give up the greenery part due to the route change in the urban part, vice versa.
 I have currently included the 11 participants who completed the same predefined routes. I had previously discussed this with Maider, and she advised focusing on within-participant comparisons rather than increasing the sample size. Her suggestion was to analyse the contrasts for Mode (Bike vs Walk) and Environment (Urban vs Green), that way each participant is compared with itself. I am not sure whether there is a strong justification for including additional participants, but I am happy to discuss this further if needed.
 In the file 11_empatica_quality_report.html I notice there are some phases look "smoother" than others. I notice it is because you forward-fill the values in _align_index.py (line 44): g.ffill(limit=18).bfill(limit=1)
   Also, in 01_empatica_build.py itself, there's already an interpolate(method='linear', limit=6) applied specifically to HR, which means it is double filled. Given that skin temperature is less temporal-sensitive/dynamic, this method should be fine. But for EDA, heart rate, and HRV, I don't think it makes sense. Please check previous studies on how they preprocess data.
 Also for 01_empatica_build.py, so far there are no frequency-domain results for HRV. However, previous work from Dr. Anna Laura Pisello [HYPERLINK: https://eur03.safelinks.protection.outlook.com/?url=https%3A%2F%2Fwww.sciencedirect.com%2Fscience%2Farticle%2Fpii%2FS0360132322006187&data=05%7C02%7Cpranav.pandya%40uclouvain.be%7C58adae5ee9d74ad4d74308dec660312a%7C7ab090d4fa2e4ecfbc7c4127b4d582ec%7C1%7C0%7C639166313594499966%7CUnknown%7CTWFpbGZsb3d8eyJFbXB0eU1hcGkiOnRydWUsIlYiOiIwLjAuMDAwMCIsIlAiOiJXaW4zMiIsIkFOIjoiTWFpbCIsIldUIjoyfQ%3D%3D%7C0%7C%7C%7C&sdata=Tk57p665WTQppKALNzKOfs3FLJ27hMtEwdQkaCk1niw%3D&reserved=0] emphasized the importance of these variables on thermal sensation. Noted that these variables are very sensitive to sampling frequency and duration (Low Frequency: 0.04 - 0.15 Hz, High Frequency: 0.15 - 0.4 Hz, and both requires a minimum of 2 - 5 minutes of continuous, artefact-free sequences), which means they cannot be computed after resampled to 0.1 hz (10 sec interval). Please check which variables are emphasized in previous studies and output them accordingly (if I remember correctly, I offered you codes that can do this task).
 You are right, I updated code to use your snippet which generates this.
  In 02_ucm_build.py, the function flag_bad_gps_points() checks if HDOP > 5 and IO_flag == 9. If not, the gps are fine and the values are saved. You computed lat, lon, time_v, and valid_coord but never used in gps drifting check. However, the trajectories look ok compared it used to be in 1-min interval. May you confirm if the gps drifting we used to believe does exist, or it was simply due to the curve of the route causing the centroid of 60 points fell out of it? 
 The gps problems are in the raw data, but this time I added this HDOP and IO_flag filters, this gives the stable trajectories seen in ucm validate html.
 I have updated the method to compute median for each 10-sec window. Please find attached ucm
 Following the previous comment, could it be better to use the medium lat&lon, or the 5[th] recording in each 10-sec window?
 The median approach improves it even more (ucm_gps_quality_comparison.ipynb notebook as attached) I will add this in ucm_build. 
 ​For 03_atom_lys_build.py, the same problem as 01_empatica_build.py, _align_index.py is called to fill in the gaps. I suggest you report the before-after difference so that we can evaluate the gap filling scheme.
 Check previous studies, such as Daylight affects human thermal perception [HYPERLINK: https://eur03.safelinks.protection.outlook.com/?url=https%3A%2F%2Fdoi.org%2F10.1038%2Fs41598-019-48963-y&data=05%7C02%7Cpranav.pandya%40uclouvain.be%7C58adae5ee9d74ad4d74308dec660312a%7C7ab090d4fa2e4ecfbc7c4127b4d582ec%7C1%7C0%7C639166313594511987%7CUnknown%7CTWFpbGZsb3d8eyJFbXB0eU1hcGkiOnRydWUsIlYiOiIwLjAuMDAwMCIsIlAiOiJXaW4zMiIsIkFOIjoiTWFpbCIsIldUIjoyfQ%3D%3D%7C0%7C%7C%7C&sdata=Tcf0t3dfbnZtdUtZTfaZ6bwQqSytdENYA4bLloSWqC0%3D&reserved=0], Influence of indoor temperature and daylight illuminance on visual perception [HYPERLINK: https://eur03.safelinks.protection.outlook.com/?url=https%3A%2F%2Fdoi.org%2F10.1177%2F1477153519859609&data=05%7C02%7Cpranav.pandya%40uclouvain.be%7C58adae5ee9d74ad4d74308dec660312a%7C7ab090d4fa2e4ecfbc7c4127b4d582ec%7C1%7C0%7C639166313594528102%7CUnknown%7CTWFpbGZsb3d8eyJFbXB0eU1hcGkiOnRydWUsIlYiOiIwLjAuMDAwMCIsIlAiOiJXaW4zMiIsIkFOIjoiTWFpbCIsIldUIjoyfQ%3D%3D%7C0%7C%7C%7C&sdata=MYVNjJcPnzo7%2BOBJzPnbTuSS7SVc0gnKEdC8CVzQFA8%3D&reserved=0], Effect of Indoor Temperature and Glazing with Saturated Color on Visual Perception of Daylight [HYPERLINK: https://eur03.safelinks.protection.outlook.com/?url=https%3A%2F%2Fdoi.org%2F10.1080%2F15502724.2020.1726182&data=05%7C02%7Cpranav.pandya%40uclouvain.be%7C58adae5ee9d74ad4d74308dec660312a%7C7ab090d4fa2e4ecfbc7c4127b4d582ec%7C1%7C0%7C639166313594542616%7CUnknown%7CTWFpbGZsb3d8eyJFbXB0eU1hcGkiOnRydWUsIlYiOiIwLjAuMDAwMCIsIlAiOiJXaW4zMiIsIkFOIjoiTWFpbCIsIldUIjoyfQ%3D%3D%7C0%7C%7C%7C&sdata=eOo3XEsgAb5i%2F0xLkpYgdENlDbjwCGKK0aZex%2FOCJD8%3D&reserved=0], to learn which LYS/light variables they used in research. I already sent these paper in the group last September when Dr. Marilyne Andersen in EPFL presented these works.
